1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,891
Important tasking gets buried in email.

2
00:00:02,891 --> 00:00:10,748
PacketRat reads the mail, finds what needs you, shows the reason and the source for every action, and sends nothing.

3
00:00:11,548 --> 00:00:16,377
PacketRat is for anyone whose important tasking gets buried in email.

4
00:00:16,377 --> 00:00:21,287
It turns an inbox into a short action queue, with the reason beside every item.

5
00:00:21,987 --> 00:00:27,892
Mail comes in three ways: exported message files, classic Outlook, or Microsoft Graph.

6
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:29,030
This demo reads files.

7
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:32,576
Outlook and Graph are built, but not verified live.

8
00:00:33,276 --> 00:00:34,805
Everything runs on this PC.

9
00:00:34,805 --> 00:00:44,334
A launcher starts a PowerShell service on loopback, a browser shows the queue, and your rules, dismissals and counts live in a local data folder.

10
00:00:45,034 --> 00:00:48,545
For judgment it calls a model, always through Fairlead.

11
00:00:48,545 --> 00:00:53,076
PacketRat holds only its own Fairlead key, and Fairlead gets OpenAI's key from Lazarette.

12
00:00:53,776 --> 00:00:54,753
The model suggests.

13
00:00:54,753 --> 00:01:00,439
Your rules decide the priority, and your own overrides and dismissals win over both.

14
00:01:00,753 --> 00:01:07,657
Out of it come drafts for review in Outlook, a counts file for Semaphore, and a backlog item for Halyard.

15
00:01:07,657 --> 00:01:11,758
PacketRat saves them; the receiving side is not tried live.

16
00:01:12,458 --> 00:01:18,317
And the rule that matters most: PacketRat never sends or deletes mail.

17
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:20,157
A person approves every reply.

18
00:01:20,957 --> 00:01:23,628
Fairlead holds the real model key.

19
00:01:23,628 --> 00:01:29,922
OpenAI's key sits in Lazarette, the vault on this PC, and is shared with Fairlead by name.

20
00:01:29,922 --> 00:01:33,057
How it gets there is in the Halyard video.

21
00:01:33,757 --> 00:01:36,194
Every tool in the fleet gets its own Fairlead key.

22
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:41,644
Pick PacketRat, install it, and it lands in Windows Credential Manager.

23
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:43,399
PacketRat holds no model key itself.

24
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:47,391
Back in PacketRat, Configuration has a Fairlead check.

25
00:01:47,391 --> 00:01:54,355
It is local only: it looks for the key in Credential Manager and contacts nothing.

26
00:01:54,355 --> 00:01:55,155
Found.

27
00:01:55,967 --> 00:01:58,310
PacketRat starts from the launcher.

28
00:01:58,310 --> 00:02:08,092
Two settings are optional: batch classification, which gets a typed record for every message, and your address, so it knows which mail is addressed to you.

29
00:02:08,792 --> 00:02:14,827
For mail it can read classic Outlook, Microsoft Graph, or exported message files.

30
00:02:14,827 --> 00:02:19,875
Here it reads fourteen invented messages from a folder, so no mailbox is on screen.

31
00:02:20,575 --> 00:02:22,324
Point it at the folder and save.

32
00:02:23,024 --> 00:02:25,191
Nothing is read until you analyze.

33
00:02:25,991 --> 00:02:26,791
Analyze inbox.

34
00:02:26,681 --> 00:02:32,335
PacketRat reads the folder and sends the messages to the model through Fairlead.

35
00:02:32,335 --> 00:02:34,232
A real model, a few seconds.

36
00:02:34,932 --> 00:02:40,732
Fourteen messages classified: eight need a reply, six need nothing.

37
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:43,333
Every claim links back to its source message.

38
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:53,389
And Fairlead's activity log shows the call: PacketRat's key, the smart route, served by OpenAI's GPT four point one.

39
00:02:54,616 --> 00:02:59,172
Overview shows the shape of the run: open actions, how many are high priority, and the next moves.

40
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:03,360
The action queue lists every follow-up with its reason.

41
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:08,931
The badge access and the same-day invoice are High: the model said so, and the rules kept it.

42
00:03:08,931 --> 00:03:10,412
The rest are Medium.

43
00:03:11,112 --> 00:03:12,788
A person overrides any priority.

44
00:03:12,788 --> 00:03:19,734
Lunch Friday is not tasking: it goes to Low, then Dismiss with a reason, and PacketRat remembers.

45
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:23,354
Dismissal memory keeps the reason, and Restore brings the item back.

46
00:03:24,054 --> 00:03:26,205
Overrides and dismissals survive the next scan.

47
00:03:27,005 --> 00:03:28,972
Draft opens a reply for any action.

48
00:03:28,972 --> 00:03:37,161
Add a line of guidance, for example: approve it, and confirm in the finance portal before fifteen hundred.

49
00:03:37,861 --> 00:03:40,010
Generate draft sends the item to the model.

50
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:45,239
One suggested reply, editable, with no signature of its own.

51
00:03:45,939 --> 00:03:46,739
Copy it.

52
00:03:46,349 --> 00:03:51,411
With Outlook mail, Open in Outlook puts it above your signature for review.

53
00:03:51,411 --> 00:03:53,627
Nothing is ever sent for you.

54
00:03:54,779 --> 00:03:56,885
The briefing lists the must-reply items.

55
00:03:56,885 --> 00:04:04,594
Each Email number is a verified source: the reference is checked against the scan, so a model cannot invent one.

56
00:04:05,294 --> 00:04:09,526
Pick a section, pick Action plan, Generate.

57
00:04:09,526 --> 00:04:14,856
The model reads only that section, and the plan is advice, not a task record.

58
00:04:15,556 --> 00:04:24,764
For the whole report, a leadership briefing: a status summary to review, marked as AI interpretation, never an authoritative record.

59
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:32,537
And every retained action keeps its source, with the sender and the reason, ready to draft or track.

60
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:35,171
Priority is explainable.

61
00:04:35,171 --> 00:04:50,743
Configuration lists the rules in order: security and mandatory action first, then direct requests from your organization, then any direct request, then replied and newsletters, which are left out.

62
00:04:50,743 --> 00:04:54,426
First match wins, and you can add your own.

63
00:04:55,006 --> 00:05:03,610
Dismissal memory keeps what you dismissed, and what the rules leave out, each with a Restore button.

64
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,054
Every button has a named command.

65
00:05:06,054 --> 00:05:11,267
A script or an agent can show the queue, or analyze, with no browser.

66
00:05:11,977 --> 00:05:17,690
Stats show counts what PacketRat did, times the minutes assumed per action.

67
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:23,528
It is an estimate, not a measurement, and not a score for anyone.

68
00:05:24,228 --> 00:05:33,157
Stats export saves the raw counts as JSON, and a small script turns them into a CSV that Semaphore or a spreadsheet can load.

69
00:05:33,857 --> 00:05:39,676
And item handoff saves a tracked mail as a backlog candidate in the format Halyard's Intake reads.

70
00:05:39,676 --> 00:05:43,068
Title, action, urgency.

71
00:05:43,068 --> 00:05:46,143
No sender, no message body.

72
00:05:47,443 --> 00:05:48,736
The same fourteen messages.

73
00:05:48,736 --> 00:05:52,667
By hand, one person took one minute twenty.

74
00:05:52,667 --> 00:05:56,499
With PacketRat, four seconds to analyze, then fifty seconds to review its queue.

75
00:05:57,199 --> 00:05:59,453
A modest saving on only fourteen messages.

76
00:05:59,453 --> 00:06:03,748
One person, one run each, not a study.

77
00:06:03,748 --> 00:06:08,966
The burden record keeps both numbers, and anyone can enter their own.

78
00:06:09,516 --> 00:06:21,113
Verified live in this recording: exported files, a real model through Fairlead, the queue, drafts, the briefing with checked sources, and the files PacketRat saves.

79
00:06:21,813 --> 00:06:31,695
Not verified live: classic Outlook, Microsoft Graph, Semaphore reading the counts, Halyard importing the hand-off.

80
00:06:31,695 --> 00:06:33,396
Minutes saved is an estimate.

81
00:06:33,396 --> 00:06:35,915
Every message here is invented.

82
00:06:36,615 --> 00:06:39,748
The authority statement is one command.

83
00:06:39,748 --> 00:06:45,548
PacketRat reads mail and proposes work, and a person decides.

84
00:06:45,548 --> 00:06:52,035
It changes mail or saved data only on a named command, and it never sends or deletes mail.

85
00:06:52,735 --> 00:06:56,272
End session stops the local service, and so does closing the tab.

86
00:06:57,072 --> 00:06:58,914
PacketRat sorts.

87
00:06:58,914 --> 00:07:00,526
People decide.

88
00:07:00,526 --> 00:07:02,252
That's the run.

89
00:07:02,252 --> 00:07:03,519
Fair winds.
